<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Emilio Aguinaldo</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1869">1869</a>–<a href="/years/1964">1964</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Emilio Aguinaldo was a leader of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippine-revolution">Philippine Revolution</a> against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> from 1896. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pact-of-biak-na-bato">Pact of Biak-na-Bato</a> he went into exile, returned in 1898 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> and declared independence on 12 June 1898. He became president of the First Philippine Republic in 1899 and led resistance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> until his capture in 1901.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/emilio-aguinaldo.jpg" alt="Emilio Aguinaldo" width="320" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Harris &amp; Ewing Photo Studio · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Emilio_Aguinaldo_ca._1919_(Restored).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Emilio Aguinaldo was born on 22 March 1869 in Cavite el Viejo, now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawit">Kawit</a>, the seventh of eight children. His father, Carlos Aguinaldo, was the community's appointed gobernadorcillo under Spanish rule, and the family was well-to-do. He studied at the Colegio de San Juan de Letran but could not finish because of a cholera outbreak in 1882. In 1895 he joined the Katipunan, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a> led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andres-bonifacio">Andrés Bonifacio</a> that sought independence by armed force, taking the name Magdalo.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>When the revolution broke out in August 1896 Aguinaldo marched from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawit">Kawit</a> with 600 men and fought the Spanish at Imus. At Binakayan and Dalahican in November his men and those of Santiago Alvarez defeated Spanish forces, a victory regarded as the first major Filipino win over a colonial power. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tejeros-convention">Tejeros Convention</a> on 22 March 1897 he was elected president of a revolutionary government, though Bonifacio disputed the result.</p><p>After Aguinaldo ordered his arrest, Bonifacio and his brother Procopio were tried and executed by firing squad on 10 May 1897. Aguinaldo says he commuted the sentence to exile and withdrew the order under pressure from generals, and that Mariano Noriel acted hastily. From 24 June 1897 he had his headquarters at Biak-na-Bato. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-american-war">Spanish–American War</a> began, Commodore George Dewey carried him from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hong-kong">Hong Kong</a> to Cavite, arriving on 19 May 1898. He promulgated the Declaration of Independence on 12 June 1898 and on 23 June set up a revolutionary government with himself as president.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The First Philippine Republic was established by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malolos-constitution">Malolos Constitution</a> on 21 January 1899 and ended when American forces captured Aguinaldo at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palanan">Palanan</a> on 23 March 1901. In the American period he largely retired from public life and organised an association of veterans of the revolution. In 1935 he ran for president against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuel-l-quezon">Manuel L. Quezon</a> and won 17.5 per cent of the vote.</p><p>After the Japanese invasion of 1941 he sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and on 1 February 1942 called on American and Filipino troops on Bataan to surrender. He was arrested in 1945 and charged with treason. He died on 6 February 1964. He is regarded in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philippines">Philippines</a> as the country's first president and a national hero who designed the flag, though his part in the deaths of Bonifacio and of General Antonio Luna remains contested.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Emilio Aguinaldo” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote><b>Emilio Aguinaldo</b>, head of the Dictatorial Government of the Philippines, commissioned it on 5 June 1898 for the proclamation of independence from Spain, to replace a march he had found unsatisfactory, and gave the task to Julián Felipe.</blockquote><a href="/songs/lupang-hinirang">Lupang Hinirang (Chosen Land)</a></li><li><blockquote>In northern Luzon, a 60-man Filipino rearguard under Brigadier General Gregorio del Pilar was overcome by more than 500 Americans, but delayed their advance long enough for President <b>Emilio Aguinaldo</b> and his troops to escape.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-2">2 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Philippine Republic was established at Malolos, Bulacan, under the Malolos Constitution, succeeding the revolutionary government, with <b>Emilio Aguinaldo</b> as its first president.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-23">23 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The federal state was headed by Roque Lopez as president, with a council representing islands such as Panay and Negros, and it acknowledged the authority of <b>Emilio Aguinaldo</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/federal-state-of-the-visayas">Federal State of the Visayas</a></li><li><blockquote>Following the Revolutionary Government of the Philippines and the drafting of the Malolos Constitution by a congress of revolutionary delegates, the Philippine Republic was inaugurated at Barasoain church in Malolos on 23 January 1899, with <b>Emilio Aguinaldo</b> as president and Apolinario Mabini as head of the cabinet.</blockquote><a href="/state/first-philippine-republic">First Philippine Republic</a></li><li><blockquote>After Spanish offensives drove the revolutionaries from Cavite in 1897, <b>Emilio Aguinaldo</b> retreated to the caves of Biak-na-Bato in San Miguel, Bulacan.</blockquote><a href="/state/republic-of-biak-na-bato">Republic of Biak-na-Bato</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kawit">Kawit</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manila">Manila</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/philippines">the Philippines</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=14.43,120.9&amp;year=1930" aria-label="Kawit on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Emilio Aguinaldo"><image href="/maps/globes/g12000.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5075 3779h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5075 3779h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=14.43,120.9&amp;year=1930">What was here in 1930? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
<section class="enc-words" id="in-their-words"><h2 class="enc-section-title">In their words</h2><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;We do not want war; on the contrary, we wish for peace; but honourable peace, which does not make you blush nor stain your forehead with shame and confusion.&#8221;</p><footer>True Version of the Philippine Revolution (1899) &#183; English edition, Tarlak, 1899 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12996/pg12996-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><blockquote class="enc-q"><p>&#8220;Go back, therefore, North American people, to your old-time liberty.&#8221;</p><footer>True Version of the Philippine Revolution (1899), to the American people &#183; English edition, Tarlak, 1899 &#183; <a href="https://www.gutenberg.org/cache/epub/12996/pg12996-images.html" rel="noopener">Project Gutenberg</a></footer></blockquote><p class="enc-article-foot"><a class="enc-random-link" href="/encyclopedia/quotations">Quotations from 595 people &rarr;</a></p></section>
